# Fix
Fixed in: b89f1ca Fix bucket delete finalizer claim recovery

The fix makes durable bucket-delete finalizer recovery claim-aware instead of
only scanning the lexicographically first Deleting bucket in each metadata PG.
The scan now returns a bounded set of finalizer roots per PG and prioritizes a
still-valid expired singleton claim's exact bucket before unrelated deleting
buckets, so stale claimed work can be rediscovered and converged after a worker
crash. Claim acquisition still preserves a different-bucket singleton claim
while that bucket is Deleting, preventing unrelated work from clearing the PG
work-class owner, but it clears an expired claim if the claimed bucket is no
longer Deleting.

The scan and acquisition paths now use consistent lease semantics:
`lease_deadline = NULL` means busy/non-expiring, while only
`lease_deadline <= now` is treated as expired recovery work. Regression coverage
was added for expired later-bucket claims hidden by an earlier deleting bucket,
different-bucket claim preservation, stale non-deleting claim cleanup, and NULL
deadline behavior.

# Summary
Introduced: production bucket-delete finalization now depends on a singleton durable claim and a durable scan that only discovers the first deleting bucket per PG. The existing different-bucket claim behavior becomes unsafe in production because the scan may never revisit the claimed bucket needed to clear an expired claim.
The commit wires bucket delete finalization to the existing durable finalizer claim table and adds a periodic scan that enqueues only one deleting bucket per metadata PG, chosen lexicographically. Claim acquisition, however, refuses to clear or steal an expired claim unless it is for the same bucket/incarnation. If a worker crashes after claiming finalization for a lexicographically later bucket, and a lexicographically earlier bucket is also or later becomes Deleting, restart recovery will always enqueue the earlier bucket. That bucket's finalizer then calls acquire_bucket_delete_finalize_claim, sees the expired claim for a different bucket, returns None/Pending, and the scan repeats the same earlier bucket forever. The claimed later bucket is never enqueued, so the expired claim is never cleared and bucket finalization for that PG can be stuck indefinitely. This is an availability/storage-reclamation issue rather than a confidentiality or integrity bypass.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the durable recovery root discovers only one Deleting bucket per metadata PG and that selection is lexicographic (`ORDER BY name ASC LIMIT 1`).
- [x] Confirm production durable scan enqueues only that single root bucket and does not enumerate other Deleting buckets on the same PG.
- [x] Confirm bucket-delete finalizer claim acquisition uses a singleton claim and refuses to clear/steal an expired claim for a different bucket/incarnation.
- [x] Confirm a failed claim acquisition is treated as Pending, so the work item is dropped and must rely on later scan rediscovery.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce the stuck state: stale claim for later bucket B, earlier bucket A becomes scan root, A claim returns None repeatedly, and B is recoverable only if directly enqueued.
## Report
Validated the finding as a production logic/availability bug, not a memory crash. Key code facts: `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:11868-11876` implements `get_bucket_delete_finalize_root()` as `WHERE state = Deleting ORDER BY name ASC LIMIT 1`, so only the lexicographically first deleting bucket in a metadata PG is returned. `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:5936-5973` calls that once per PG and enqueues only `root.bucket`. `crates/storage/src/pg_store.rs:12183-12199` loads the singleton `bucket_delete_finalize_claims` row and, after the lease is expired, still returns `Ok(None)` if the existing claim is for different bucket/incarnation (`!same_work`). `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:2102-2108` converts `None` to `BucketDeleteFinalizeOutcome::Pending`, dropping that work item until a later scan. I added a targeted unit-test PoC in `poc_test.patch`: bucket B is marked Deleting and receives a claim with lease deadline 20; after time 21, lexicographically earlier bucket A is marked Deleting. The scan root remains A, but `acquire_bucket_delete_finalize_claim(A, ...)` returns `None` twice because the stale singleton claim belongs to B. A direct claim for B then succeeds with `attempt_count == 2`, proving the stale claim would be recoverable only if B were rediscovered. Command evidence: `cargo test -p storage poc_stale_later_bucket_claim_blocks_lexicographic_scan_root -- --nocapture` completed with `running 1 test ... ok`. I also attempted crash-style execution; the PoC does not crash because the bug is logical. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). I used LLDB non-interactively with a breakpoint at `pg_store.rs:12197`; it stopped in `acquire_bucket_delete_finalize_claim` from the PoC with `same_work = false`, `claim_id = claim-a-new`, `now = 21`, and an existing claim record from the earlier B claim, confirming the expired different-bucket path that returns `None`.

